1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Why don't you talk to your parents anymore?

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,200
My parents discovered I wasn't their biological child when I was 16.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,040
Not through some dramatic DNA test.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:08,720
Through my little sister's science project,

5
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:11,600
she was doing genetics homework, mapping out eye colors,

6
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,120
and couldn't make our family tree work.

7
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:16,480
Brown eyes plus brown eyes can't make blue eyes, she said, pointing at me.

8
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:17,200
Mom went white.

9
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,280
Dad started stuttering about recessive genes.

10
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:21,280
That night, I heard them screaming through the walls.

11
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,520
Mom admitted she'd had an affair 17 years ago.

12
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:24,560
Dad wasn't my father.

13
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,480
The man who raised me, taught me to ride a bike,

14
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,240
helped with homework for 16 years,

15
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,920
suddenly looked at me like I was a stranger.

16
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:31,600
The change was immediate and brutal.

17
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,360
Dad stopped coming to my baseball games.

18
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,400
When I'd try talking to him, he'd respond with one word answers,

19
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,280
or just leave the room.

20
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:39,120
Mom kept insisting nothing had changed,

21
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,120
but she'd flinch whenever Dad was cold to me.

22
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,480
Then came the comparisons.

23
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:45,600
Every family dinner became a catalog of how my sister and I were different.

24
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,600
Look how naturally athletic Sophie is.

25
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,520
Dad would say, watching her throw a ball.

26
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:50,400
She gets that from me.

27
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,320
The implication hung in the air like poison.

28
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:53,920
They started treating me like a house guest.

29
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,240
My curfew got earlier while my sisters stayed the same.

30
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:58,240
My allowance got cut because money's tight,

31
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:00,160
but Sophie got new clothes whenever she asked.

32
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,320
When I brought it up, Mom said I was being dramatic.

33
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,320
The worst part was watching them rewrite history.

34
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:06,480
Photos of me got moved to the back of albums.

35
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,480
My baby pictures disappeared from the mantle.

36
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:10,960
Dad started telling stories about Sophie's milestones,

37
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,560
while pretending mine never happened.

38
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:14,560
It was like they were erasing 16 years.

39
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,320
My breaking point came during Christmas.

40
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:17,520
Extended family was over,

41
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:19,600
and Uncle Mike asked about my college plans.

42
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:20,160
Dad said,

43
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:22,080
Well, he's not really academic like Sophie.

44
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,120
Different genetics, you know.

45
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:24,880
He said genetics like it was a dirty word.

46
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:25,680
That's when it clicked.

47
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,440
They didn't just learn I wasn't blood related.

48
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:28,960
They decided I wasn't family,

49
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,560
so I made a choice that destroyed them.

50
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,080
I found my biological father.

51
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,440
His name was John Sins.

52
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:36,320
He lived three states away, had no idea I existed.

53
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,480
When I called him, he cried, actually cried,

54
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,200
said he'd wondered about my mom for years, hoped she was happy.

55
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,840
Two weeks later, John drove 400 miles to meet me.

56
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:45,440
He brought photo albums of his family,

57
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,920
told me about my half-brother who played baseball like me,

58
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:49,680
showed me pictures of my real grandmother

59
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:50,960
who had died the year before.

60
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:52,240
But here's what broke my parents.

61
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:54,240
John didn't just accept me, he celebrated me.

62
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,080
Within a month, he'd driven back twice more.

63
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,000
He came to my games, met my friends,

64
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,080
took me car shopping for my 17th birthday.

65
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:01,600
My parents watched this man give me

66
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:02,800
everything they'd taken away.

67
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,960
Dad became obsessed, calling John a stranger

68
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,160
trying to steal his son.

69
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,600
Mom begged me to stop seeing him,

70
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:08,960
said it was destroying our family.

71
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,400
I told them they'd already destroyed it.

72
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,000
The night they decided biology mattered

73
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,600
more than 16 years of love.

74
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,280
The final blow came at graduation.

75
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:16,400
Both my families showed up.

76
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:17,760
John sat in the front row cheering.

